Day 12 of my DevOps Troubleshooting Journey

🔴 Real-Time Scenario: Kubernetes Pods Restarting Frequently

Imagine users report that the application is intermittently unavailable. You check Kubernetes and find that some application pods are restarting again and again.

As a DevOps Engineer, what would you check?

🔍 My Troubleshooting Approach:

1️⃣ Check Pod Status

```bash
kubectl get pods -n <namespace>
```

Look for:
• CrashLoopBackOff
• Error status
• High restart count
• OOMKilled

2️⃣ Describe the Pod

```bash
kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>
```

Check the Events section for failed probes, container failures, OOMKilled, and other errors.

3️⃣ Check Application Logs

```bash
kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace>
kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace> --previous
```

The `--previous` option is especially useful when the container has already restarted.

4️⃣ Check Resource Usage

```bash
kubectl top pod -n <namespace>
kubectl top nodes
```

This helps identify CPU or memory-related issues.

5️⃣ Check Deployment Configuration

Verify:
✅ CPU & memory requests/limits
✅ Liveness & readiness probes
✅ Environment variables
✅ ConfigMaps & Secrets
✅ Application configuration

🛠️ Possible Root Causes:

• Application crash or configuration issue
• Memory limit exceeded
• Incorrect environment variables
• Database/external service connection failure
• Failed health probes
• Insufficient node resources

💡 Key Learning:

Don't just restart the pods and consider the issue resolved.

First identify WHY the container is restarting, fix the root cause, and then monitor the application to make sure the pods remain healthy.

This is the kind of systematic troubleshooting approach I am building as part of my DevOps learning journey.
